<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" class="scroll-smooth">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
{# Every page's <head> metadata is written here and nowhere else.

   A page contributes what it knows about itself by setting `page_title`,
   `page_description` and `page_type` before this layout renders; everything it
   leaves unset falls through to the configured default ([24.4]). Pages state
   plain text — the escaping happens once, on the way out, here.

   The single writer matters most for the indexing directive. Four funnel pages
   used to hard-code a <meta name="robots"> of their own on top of the one this
   layout rendered from a controller variable, so the same decision was written
   twice with two different values. It now comes from the robots policy, which
   is also what the response header and the sitemap are derived from ([24.2],
   [24.6]). `noindex` stays available as a page-level escalation for a page that
   knows it must not be indexed for a reason its path cannot express — the error
   pages are exactly that — but it can only refuse indexing, never grant it. #}
{% set page_meta = seo.page.with(page_title|default(null), page_description|default(null), page_type|default(null)) %}
{% set page_robots = (noindex is defined and noindex) ? seo.refused : page_meta.robots %}
<title>{{ page_meta.title }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ page_meta.description }}" />
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ page_meta.canonical }}" />
{% if page_robots %}
<meta name="robots" content="{{ page_robots }}" />
{% endif %}
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ page_meta.social.type }}" />
<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ page_meta.social.site_name }}" />
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ page_meta.social.title }}" />
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ page_meta.social.description }}" />
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ page_meta.social.url }}" />
{# A deployment with no social image renders no image tag at all: a card
   pointing at an image that does not exist is what a crawler caches. #}
{% if page_meta.social.image %}
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ page_meta.social.image }}" />
{% endif %}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="{{ page_meta.social.image ? 'summary_large_image' : 'summary' }}" />
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ page_meta.social.title }}" />
<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ page_meta.social.description }}" />
{% if page_meta.social.image %}
<meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ page_meta.social.image }}" />
{% endif %}
{# For anything a single page needs on top of the above. Nothing that belongs
   to every page goes in here — that is what the block above is for. #}
{% block meta %}{% endblock %}
{# The page's schema.org document, which renders nothing at all unless the page
   published one and the deployment left its emitter switched on ([24.8]).

   Not on a page that escalated to `noindex` on its own account. The document
   is assembled from the path alone, so an error page — served at any URL at
   all — was being handed the site-level Organization/WebSite graph: site
   identity rather than a false claim about the missing page, so noise rather
   than a lie, but noise a crawler may act on. The escalation is the right
   condition and the resolved directive is not: with [24.7]'s master switch on,
   every page carries `noindex` and gating on that would take structured data
   off the whole site. `noindex` set by the template is narrower — it means
   "this is not a document", which is exactly and only what the three error
   pages say about themselves. Suppressed here rather than in the partial
   because this layout is where the head's metadata decisions are made; the
   partial stays a renderer of whatever it is given. #}
{% if not (noindex is defined and noindex) %}
{% include 'partials/structured-data.twig' ignore missing %}
{% endif %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('app.css') }}" />
<script src="{{ asset('lucide.min.js') }}" defer></script>
</head>
<body class="bg-background font-body text-heading antialiased{% block body_class %}{% endblock %}">
{# `page` defaults to rendering `content` directly so templates extending base.twig
   without a marketing/funnel/checkout layout (e.g. the error pages) keep working
   unchanged; marketing/funnel/checkout layouts override `page` wholesale with
   header + content + footer. #}
{% block page %}{% block content %}{% endblock %}{% endblock %}
{# Lucide's script is `defer`red (see <head>), so icons must be created after
   DOMContentLoaded rather than inline at the end of <body> as the mockup does —
   deviation for correctness, visual result identical. #}
<script>window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () { if (window.lucide) lucide.createIcons(); });</script>
{% block scripts %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
